{
  "provider": "azure",
  "domain": "virtual-machine-sizes",
  "updated_at": "2026-08-16",
  "coverage": "Deliberately partial. Azure publishes one page per VM series and there are well over a hundred series, so this table holds the ten that generated and hand written terraform actually reaches for: the burstable families on both silicon vendors, the general purpose and memory optimised v4, v5 and v6 families, and the FX compute optimised pair. A size that is not here has no encoded ceiling and every rule that would need one stays quiet. The uncached figures are the ceiling a size imposes on all of its uncached disks added together, read from the Remote storage tab of the series page and never from the Local storage tab, which publishes NVMe numbers that have nothing to do with a managed disk. Where a series publishes a second uncached pair for Ultra Disk and Premium SSD v2, it is carried separately in the uncached_v2 fields, because those disks are governed by the higher pair and judging them against the Premium SSD pair would understate the ceiling, which is how a rule invents a finding.",
  "burst_notes": "Azure B-series has no equivalent of the AWS T3 'unlimited' mode. There is no setting, no surcharge and no escape: when the credit balance reaches zero the virtual machine is throttled to its base CPU performance and stays there for as long as the load continues. That makes the B-series ceiling a latency cliff rather than a bill, which is the opposite of the AWS default and the reason this rule cannot be a copy of R7. Base CPU performance is expressed on a 0-100% scale across the whole VM since the 2024 renormalisation: a 2 vCPU size at 40% sustains 0.8 vCPUs, not 0.8 per core.",
  "storage_notes": "Uncached IOPS and MB/s are the ceiling the VM imposes on every data disk attached with host caching set to None, added together. Disks attached with ReadOnly or ReadWrite caching are governed by a separate cached limit that this table does not carry, so a rule must only compare uncached disks against these numbers. Burst figures are the VM's, and both the disk and the VM have to be bursting at once for the burst number to be reachable.",
  "series": {
    "Basv2": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/general-purpose/basv2-series",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-16",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "AMD burstable. Not interchangeable with the Intel Bsv2: every size from 2 to 16 vCPUs matches, and the two 32 vCPU sizes do not. Standard_B32as_v2 stops at 25,600 uncached IOPS where Standard_B32s_v2 reaches 51,200."
    },
    "Bsv2": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/general-purpose/bsv2-series",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-14",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "The current burstable family. Same credit model as Bv1 with a cleaner baseline ladder and much higher uncached disk limits."
    },
    "Bv1": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/general-purpose/bv1-series",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-14",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "Previous generation, still the most common thing a model writes. The Microsoft page prints the size as 'Standard_B1ls2' in the basics and storage tables and 'Standard_B1ls' in the credit table; the trailing 2 is a footnote marker, and B1ls is the real name. B1ls is Linux only."
    },
    "Dsv5": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/general-purpose/dsv5-series",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-14",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "No local temp disk."
    },
    "Easv5": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/memory-optimized/easv5-series?tabs=sizestorageremote",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-16",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "AMD. No local temp disk. Same uncached IOPS as the Intel Esv5 at each vCPU count and different throughput: Standard_E8as_v5 drives 200 MB/s against 290 for Standard_E8s_v5."
    },
    "Esv4": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/memory-optimized/esv4-series?tabs=sizestorageremote",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-16",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "No local temp disk. The uncached ceilings are materially lower than Esv5 at the same vCPU count: Standard_E8s_v4 drives 192 MB/s where Standard_E8s_v5 drives 290. Reading a v5 row for a v4 size overstates the ceiling."
    },
    "Esv5": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/memory-optimized/esv5-series",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-14",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "No local temp disk. Memory sizes are printed as GB on the Microsoft page and as GiB elsewhere; recorded as printed."
    },
    "Esv6": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/memory-optimized/esv6-series?tabs=sizestorageremote",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-16",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "No local temp disk. Memory is printed as GB on the Microsoft page and recorded as printed, the same treatment Esv5 gets. This series publishes two uncached column pairs, one for Premium SSD and one for Ultra Disk and Premium SSD v2; uncached_iops and uncached_mbps carry the Premium SSD pair and the uncached_v2 fields carry the other."
    },
    "FXmdsv2": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/compute-optimized/fxmdsv2-series?tabs=sizestorageremote",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-16",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "Local NVMe present, and its Local storage tab publishes far larger figures than the remote one: Standard_FX8mds_v2 shows 150,000 local read IOPS against 33,000 uncached remote. Only the remote numbers belong here, because a managed disk is remote storage. This series publishes a second uncached pair for Ultra Disk and Premium SSD v2, carried in the uncached_v2 fields."
    },
    "FXmsv2": {
      "source": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes/compute-optimized/fxmsv2-series?tabs=sizestorageremote",
      "verified_at": "2026-08-16",
      "confidence": "high",
      "notes": "The same series without local storage. Its remote storage table is identical to FXmdsv2 line for line, and it publishes the same second uncached pair for Ultra Disk and Premium SSD v2."
    }
  },
